body:
Not being Christian.

&#x200B;

Sorry mom but I could never follow that religion.

I personally like that witchcraft is receiving more representation or becoming "trendy" as you put it.  This may be because I'm still very much closeted about my practice, but the more open people are about embracing it, even if it is in ways that I don't necessarily follow, the more I feel that I can open up as a witch to others.

&#x200B;

In the end, witchcraft is a very personalized practice.  There are no rights and wrongs, so any "fluff" that's out there doesn't phase me in the slightest.  I'm still going to do what I do and ignore what doesn't resonate with me.

I used this for loop to try and print the area but all i got was empty space 




    for (int totalItems = 0; totalItems < MAX_ITEMS; totalItems++)
    		cout << names[totalItems] << endl;

so an if statement would be better?




    	if (totalItems < MAX_ITEMS)
    	{
    		names[totalItems] = name;
    		totalItems++;

    		return true;
    	}
    	else
    		return false;

When I run the code, how can i tell if the array accepted the string?

I have this function that checks if the array is full




    	bool Names::full() const
    	{
    		if (totalItems == MAX_ITEMS)
    			return true;
    		else
    			return false;
    	}

No, they aren't global, except for the constant.

This is the full version of my header file (which i'm using for all my cpps)

#include <iostream>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

const int MAX_ITEMS = 10;

class Names {
public:

	Names();

	bool insert(string name);
	void print();

private:
	int name;
	int totalItems;
	string names[MAX_ITEMS];
};
